.TH QEMU\-TOOL 1 "November 2026" "qemu-tool 1.7.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
qemu\-tool \- generate, run and inspect QEMU guests and their compose stacks
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qemu\-tool
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ options ]
.PP
.I COMMAND
is one of
.BR quick\-start ,
.BR check ,
.BR gen\-vm ,
.BR run\-vm ,
.BR gen\-compose ,
.BR compose ,
.BR down ,
.BR list ,
.BR inspect ,
.BR ssh ,
.BR qcow ,
.BR publish\-qcow ,
.B ernic\-stats
or
.BR man .
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B qemu\-tool
generates, runs and inspects QEMU guests, and the
.B docker compose
stacks that put emulated GPUs, RDMA NICs and SDXI devices behind them.
Settings come from fleet XML files; see
.BR "SETTINGS FILE" .
.SS Start here
.TP
.B quick\-start
Boot the default stack sized for this host \(em two guests, each with an
emulated GPU, an NVMe, an RDMA NIC and an SDXI device, with Prometheus,
Grafana and log collection beside them \(em then run a GPU test on each guest
and an RDMA test across them. Every image is pulled, the guest's included;
nothing is built on this host. This is the fastest path to
a working stack and the command the daily benchmark lane runs.
.TP
.B check
Ask every host question the stacks depend on and report the answers: the
tools on
.IR PATH ,
the Docker daemon and your access to it, KVM, the uid the containers will run
as, which settings file is in force, and whether the images directory has
room. Read-only, needs no
.BR sudo (8),
and writes nothing, so it is safe on a host you have not decided to use yet.
Exits non-zero if anything would stop a stack starting; warnings do not fail
it. The same checks
.B quick\-start
runs in its preflight, so the two cannot drift.
.SS One guest
.TP
.B gen\-vm
Create a guest image: bake one from an Ubuntu cloud image with cloud-init, a
package manifest and optionally an Ansible playbook, or overlay an image that
already exists or is published in a registry.
.TP
.B run\-vm
Launch a guest created by
.BR gen\-vm .
.SS A stack of guests
.TP
.B gen\-compose
Render a compose stack \(em N guests, each with its rocm-ernic NICs, rocjitsu
GPUs and SDXI devices, and the mesh between them \(em from fleet XML.
.TP
.B compose
Run
.B docker compose
against a stack, with the caller's identity and the images directory supplied
through the environment.
.TP
.B down
Gracefully shut down guests and, optionally, tear down the containing stack.
With no arguments, asks every running guest to power off through the QEMU
guest agent and waits for it to go. With a VM name, shuts down that guest.
With a stack name (the
.B STACK
column in
.BR list ),
shuts down that stack's guests then tears the stack down.
With
.BR \-\-all ,
shuts down every guest then tears down every stack you own.
.SS What is running
.TP
.B list
List every QEMU VM running on this node, including VMs inside containers, and
identify which of them
.B qemu\-tool
started.
See
.BR "VM IDENTIFICATION" .
.TP
.B inspect
Report how to log in to a running guest and what devices it has.
.TP
.B ssh
Open an SSH session to a running guest.
.TP
.B qcow
Inspect and manage VM overlay images. Lists all overlays with their sizes and
COW delta, shows the full overlay state for one VM, or deletes and recreates a
stopped VM's overlay from its backing image.
.SS Everything else
.TP
.B publish\-qcow
Publish a baked guest qcow2 to a registry as the artifact
.B gen\-vm \-\-backing\-image
and
.B quick\-start
pull.
.TP
.B ernic\-stats
Serve the stats files written by
.B "rocm\-ernic \-S"
as Prometheus metrics. Runs inside the compose stack; rarely invoked by hand.
.TP
.B man
Show this page.
.PP
VM images are stored in
.I /var/lib/qemu-tool/images
by default.
The directory is owned by root and group\-writable by the
.B kvm
group.
Users must be a member of the
.B kvm
group to read or write images without
.BR sudo .
.SH SHARED OPTIONS
These options are accepted by both
.B run\-vm
and
.BR gen\-vm .
.TP
.BI \-\-xml " FILE"
Read settings from the fleet XML file
.I FILE
instead of searching the default locations.
.B Repeatable:
give it more than once and the files are layered left to right, an element
set by a later file overriding the same element in an earlier one and an
element absent from the later file keeping the earlier value. That is what
lets one shipped fleet file carry everything a fleet shares while a small
overlay states only the handful of elements that differ. See
.B SETTINGS FILE
below.
.TP
.BI \-\-vm\-name " NAME"
Name of the VM (default:
.IR qemu-minimal ).
Used to derive image file paths such as
.IR <images>/<NAME>.qcow2 .
May not contain a comma, which would terminate the QEMU option value it is
substituted into.
.TP
.BI \-\-arch " ARCH"
Target architecture:
.BR amd64 ,
.BR arm64 ,
or
.BR riscv64
(default:
.IR amd64 ).
.TP
.BI \-\-vcpus " N"
Number of virtual CPUs (default: 2).
.TP
.BI \-\-vmem " MiB"
Amount of guest RAM in MiB (default: 4096).
.TP
.BI \-\-images " DIR"
Directory where VM image files are stored (default:
.IR /var/lib/qemu-tool/images ).
.TP
.BI \-\-ssh\-port " PORT"
Host port forwarded to guest SSH (default: 2222).
.TP
.BI \-\-mac " ADDR"
Management NIC MAC address.
By default it is derived from
.BR \-\-ssh\-port
as
.IR 52:54:00:00:<hi>:<lo> .
Set it when the guest image pins its netplan to a particular MAC. Images
built by
.B gen\-vm
since
.I 3969f07
match on interface name instead and do not need this; images it built before
that commit, and images built elsewhere, may still pin one. Run
.I "grep \-A4 ethernets /etc/netplan/50\-cloud\-init.yaml"
in the guest to see what it pinned.
.TP
.BR \-\-kvm " | " \-\-no\-kvm
Enable or disable KVM hardware acceleration (default: enabled).
.TP
.BI \-\-qemu\-path " PATH"
Override the QEMU binary path.
By default the appropriate
.B qemu-system-*
binary is located via
.BR PATH .
.TP
.BI \-\-domain " FILE"
Load a libvirt domain XML file as the base configuration.
Use
.B \-
to read from stdin.
CLI flags override values from the XML.
.SH QUICK-START OPTIONS
.B quick\-start
takes one optional verb:
.BR all " (the default), " preflight ", " up ", " test ", " report ", " logs " or " down .
.B preflight
checks this machine and touches nothing;
.B up
stops once the guests are booted;
.B test
configures and tests a stack that is already up;
.B down
stops it and leaves the overlays in place, so a later run skips the pull.
.IP
Anything still unknown is asked for on stdin. Every prompt has an answer it
takes on its own when stdin is not a terminal, so a CI run needs no flags to
stay non\-interactive \(em and the chosen answer is printed either way.
.TP
.BI \-\-xml " FILE"
Fleet XML settings file. Defaults to the flavor's own file \(em
.I qemu/stacks/rocm\-golden.xml
for the default flavor \(em which ships inside the wheel and the Debian
package, so this works with no checkout. Repeatable; a later file overrides
the elements it sets.
.TP
.BI \-\-stack\-dir " DIR"
Where the stack is generated. Default
.IR /var/lib/qemu\-tool/stacks/quickstart ,
falling back to a directory under
.B $XDG_STATE_HOME
when that is not writable \(em which on a fresh host it is not.
.TP
.BI \-\-key\-dir " DIR"
Where the guest keypair is pulled to. Default
.IR /var/lib/qemu\-tool/quickstart\-key ,
falling back to a directory under
.B $XDG_DATA_HOME
when that path is not writable \(em which on a fresh host it is not.
The keypair ships as an ORAS referrer beside the guest qcow2, not inside it.
.TP
.BI \-\-images " DIR"
Directory for the guest qcow2 files, overriding
.BR <vm\-images\-dir> .
Without it an unwritable default is asked about up front rather than failing
part way into the pull.
.TP
.BI \-\-report\-dir " DIR"
Write a markdown report here, and run the report step.
.TP
.BI \-\-repo\-root " DIR"
The checkout holding
.IR ansible/playbooks/vm\-quickstart.yml .
Found automatically from a source install. An installed tool with no checkout
brings the guests up and skips the configuration and the tests, saying so.
.TP
.B \-\-size\-from\-host
Derive
.BR <vm\-vcpus> ", " <vm\-vmem> " and " <vm\-shm\-size>
from this machine, written to an
.I overlay\-sized.xml
layer beside the stack rather than into the settings file.
.BR "On by default" ;
.B \-\-no\-size\-from\-host
uses the values as written. The floors still apply: 4 vCPU, below which
.B ionic_rdma
never probes, and 4 GiB.
.TP
.B \-\-metrics
Bring Prometheus and Grafana up with the stack, with their data directories
beside it so neither needs root or a chown.
.BR "On by default" ;
.B \-\-no\-metrics
leaves both out.
.TP
.B \-\-in\-network
Reach the guests on the compose network rather than on published host ports.
For a caller that is itself a container attached to that network, which is
what the CI lane is.
.TP
.BR \-\-yes ", " \-y
Take the default answer to every prompt without asking.
.TP
.BI \-\-flavor " NAME"
Quickstart flavor to run.
.RS
.TP
.B default
Two guests each with an emulated gfx1250 GPU, one NVMe, one rocm-ernic NIC
with P4 in guest scope and one SDXI device, driven by the bundled
.IR qemu/stacks/rocm\-golden.xml :
the rocm\-golden fleet. Every image it names is pulled.
This is the default when
.B \-\-flavor
is omitted.
.TP
.B mi355x
Four VMs, each with 8 GPUs, 8 ernics and 8 PCIe switches in a switched topology,
driven by the bundled
.IR qemu/stacks/mi355x\-server.xml .
The ansible configure-and-test step does not run for this flavor.
.TP
.B helios
One VM with 4 gfx1250 GPUs, 8 ernics and one SDXI device, driven by the
bundled
.IR qemu/stacks/helios\-tray.xml .
The ansible configure-and-test step does not run for this flavor.
.RE
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-follow
Follow log output when running the
.B logs
verb (equivalent to
.BR "docker compose logs \-f" ).
.TP
.BI \-\-rdma\-duration " SECONDS"
Run
.B ib_send_bw
for this many seconds instead of the default 1000 iterations. Useful for a
longer soak test of the RDMA mesh.
.TP
.BI \-\-nvme\-duration " SECONDS"
Run each
.B fio
NVMe pass for this many seconds. Default: 10.
.TP
.B \-\-clean\-overlays
Delete the guest overlay and NVMe qcow2 files before starting, so the guests
boot from a fresh overlay of the backing image. The backing file itself is
kept. Only meaningful with the
.B up
or
.B all
verbs.
.SH RUN-VM OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-filesystem " DIR"
Expose a host directory to the guest via virtio-9p.
.TP
.B \-\-vfio\-user\-root\-port
Place vfio\-user devices behind a PCIe root port.
.BR "On by default" ;
.B \-\-no\-vfio\-user\-root\-port
asks for the old topology, as does
.B <vm\-vfio\-user\-root\-port>false</vm\-vfio\-user\-root\-port>
in a settings file.
.IP
Without a root port the devices land on the root bus as conventional PCI
endpoints with no Express capability and a 32\-bit prefetchable BAR. For the
Ethernet path that costs only the reported link speed \(em
.I 0.000 Gb/s available PCIe bandwidth (Unknown x255 link)
from
.IR ionic .
For a GPU it is fatal. Measured on the emulated rocjitsu, same guest and
image, with only this changed:
.PP
.RS
.nf
off: Region 0: fd000000   (32\-bit, prefetchable) [size=16M]
on:  Region 0: c040000000 (64\-bit, prefetchable) [size=256M]
     Capabilities: [40] Express (v2) Endpoint
.fi
.RE
.PP
16M is far too small for a VRAM aperture, so
.IR amdgpu 's
memory controller cannot initialise and the probe unwinds with \-22. The
module still loads, so nothing looks wrong locally, no KFD node appears, and
the first complaint arrives from HIP as
.IR "no ROCm\-capable device is detected" .
.BR gen\-compose
warns when a stack renders GPUs with this turned off, because that message
names nothing about the cause.
.IP
It is necessary and not sufficient: 256M is still short of a gfx1250
aperture, and the remaining limit is the rocjitsu device model's BAR size.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-vfio\-user\-root\-port
Keep vfio\-user devices on the root bus as conventional endpoints. A GPU will
not bind
.IR amdgpu .
.TP
.BI \-\-vfio\-user\-switch\-width " N"
Group vfio\-user devices under PCIe switches,
.I N
per switch, in
.B \-\-vfio\-userdev
order. One
.B pcie\-root\-port
per switch, an
.B x3130\-upstream
on it, and an
.B xio3130\-downstream
per device. 0, the default, is off and leaves the flat tree.
.IP
Order is the pairing: nothing inspects what a socket serves, so the caller
decides what shares a switch by the order it lists the sockets. The
MI355X\-shaped stack interleaves its NIC and GPU sockets and asks for width
2, so each switch carries one of each and their traffic has a path that does
not cross the root complex.
.IP
Not representable in libvirt XML:
.B \-\-convert\-to\-libvirt
refuses a switched configuration rather than emitting a domain with a flat
tree.
.TP
.BI \-\-cpu\-sockets " N"
Pin the guest socket count. Without it QEMU infers
.IR sockets=cpus ", " cores=1 ,
so a guest given 8 vCPUs reports eight single\-core sockets. Must divide
.BR \-\-vcpus .
.TP
.BI \-\-cpu\-phys\-bits " N"
Physical address bits for the EPYC CPU. Default 40 (1\~TiB address space).
Raise to 41 (2\~TiB) alongside
.B \-\-pci\-hole64\-size
when a large BAR would exceed the default space; both must move together.
QEMU refuses a hole larger than the address space allows at startup rather
than at boot time.
.TP
.BI \-\-pci\-hole64\-size " SIZE"
Size of the 64\-bit PCI hole as a QEMU size string, e.g.\&
.IR 512G .
The machine default does not fit many large BARs at once; eight emulated
GPUs will not enumerate without this.
.IP
Bounded by
.BR \-\-cpu\-phys\-bits :
.B \-cpu EPYC
gives 40 bits (1\~TiB) by default, so on QEMU 10.2.1
.I 512G
is accepted and
.I 1T
is not without raising
.BR \-\-cpu\-phys\-bits .
.TP
.B \-\-mgmt\-tap
Use tap+bridge for the management NIC instead of SLIRP.
.TP
.BI \-\-nvme " VALUE"
Attach NVMe storage.
A positive integer creates that many QCOW2-backed NVMe drives.
A negative integer creates
.B null_blk
devices.
Any other string is passed as literal NVMe device arguments.
.TP
.BI \-\-nvme\-trace " {doorbell,all,EVENT}"
Enable QEMU NVMe trace for the specified event category.
.TP
.BI \-\-nvme\-trace\-file " FILE"
Write NVMe trace output to
.I FILE
instead of stderr.
.TP
.BI \-\-nvme\-lbaf\-mask " HEX"
Restrict the advertised LBA format mask (hex bitmask).
.TP
.B \-\-nvme\-recreate
Recreate NVMe image files even if they already exist.
.TP
.BI \-\-nvme\-dbbuf\-poll\-ns " NS"
Shadow-doorbell poll period in nanoseconds.
When non-zero, the NVMe controller polls the shadow doorbell at this interval
rather than waiting for a CPU MMIO write, enabling GPU-initiated I/O without
CPU involvement.
.B 0
(the default) keeps the existing event-driven behaviour.
Only emitted when the QEMU binary supports the property.
.TP
.BI \-\-nvme\-dbbuf\-poll\-eventidx " {on,off}"
Shadow-doorbell event-index mode.
.B off
tells the controller it never needs to ring the host doorbell via MMIO,
removing the last CPU write on the submission path.
Only emitted when the QEMU binary supports the property.
.TP
.BI \-\-nvme\-vram\-dev " ID"
qdev id of a vfio-user device (e.g.\&
.IR rocjitsu\-4\-1 )
to use as the VRAM source for NVMe P2P DMA.
Applied to the first NVMe controller created by
.BR \-\-nvme " N" .
Requires the QEMU binary to support
.BR vram\-dev= .
.TP
.BI \-\-nvme\-vram\-bar " N"
BAR index on the vfio-user device identified by
.BR \-\-nvme\-vram\-dev
(default: 0).
.TP
.B \-\-pci\-testdev
Attach the QEMU
.B pci-testdev
device.
.TP
.BI \-\-pci\-hostdev " BDF[,BDF]"
Pass through a host PCI device via VFIO.
Accepts a PCI BDF address (e.g.\&
.IR 0000:03:00.0 ).
May be repeated or comma-separated for multiple devices.
.TP
.BI \-\-vram\-dev\-index " N"
1-based index into the
.B \-\-pci\-hostdev
list identifying the GPU device for VRAM BAR mapping.
.TP
.BI \-\-vram\-bar " N"
BAR index for VRAM mapping (default: 0).
.TP
.BI \-\-vfio\-userdev " SOCK[,SOCK]"
Attach a vfio-user device via its Unix socket path.
May be repeated or comma-separated.
.TP
.B \-\-pci\-mmio\-bridge
Add a PCI-to-PCI bridge for extended MMIO window.
.TP
.BI \-\-data\-nic\-queues " N"
Number of virtio-net queues for the data NIC (default: 0, single-queue).
.TP
.BI \-\-mcast\-group " IP:PORT"
Attach a second NIC to the specified UDP multicast group.
.TP
.BI \-\-qmp\-socket " [PATH]"
Enable the QMP monitor socket.
Without an argument an automatic path under
.I /tmp
is used.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-qemu\-guest\-agent
Disable the QEMU guest agent channel.
.TP
.B \-\-backing\-shared
Mark the backing image as shared so multiple VMs can run from the same base.
.TP
.BI \-\-extra\-hostfwd " RULE"
Append an additional SLIRP host-forward rule, e.g.\&
.IR tcp::9150\-:9100 .
May be repeated.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the QEMU command that would be executed and exit without running it.
.TP
.BI \-\-convert\-to\-libvirt " [FILE]"
Emit a libvirt domain XML description instead of running the VM.
Write to
.I FILE
(default:
.IR <vm-name>.xml ),
or to stdout when
.B \-
is given.
.SH GEN-VM OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-mgmt\-tap
Use tap+bridge for the management NIC instead of SLIRP.
.TP
.BI \-\-size " GB"
Disk size in GB (default: 64).
.TP
.BI \-\-release " NAME"
Ubuntu release codename (e.g.\&
.IR resolute )
or version (e.g.\&
.IR 26.04 )
(default:
.IR resolute ).
.TP
.BI \-\-ssh\-key\-file " FILE"
Public SSH key to inject into the guest (default:
.IR ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub ).
.TP
.BI \-\-username " NAME"
Guest user account name (default:
.IR ubuntu ).
.TP
.BI \-\-user\-id " UID"
UID for the guest user account (default: 1000).
.TP
.BI \-\-password " PASS"
Guest user password (default:
.IR password ).
.TP
.BI \-\-packages " FILE"
Path to a cloud-init package manifest.
Pass
.B none
to skip package installation.
Default:
.IR /usr/share/qemu-tool/packages-default .
.TP
.B \-\-force
Overwrite an existing VM image.
.TP
.BR \-\-compact " | " \-\-no\-compact
Recompress the backing image with
.I "qemu\-img convert \-c"
once the bake finishes (default: enabled).
Compression is safe on a backing file because guest writes land in the
overlay and never rewrite a compressed cluster.
Skipped when
.B \-\-no\-backing
is set.
Use
.B \-\-no\-compact
for a fast local rebuild loop.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-backing
Create a single flat image rather than a backing/overlay pair.
.TP
.B \-\-restore\-image
Re-create the overlay from the backing image, discarding current VM state.
.TP
.BI \-\-backing\-file " FILE"
Use an existing image as the backing file instead of downloading a cloud image.
.TP
.BI \-\-ansible\-playbook " FILE"
Run Ansible against the VM after first boot using the specified playbook.
.TP
.BI \-\-ca\-cert " FILE"
Inject a CA certificate into the guest trust store.
.TP
.B \-\-ansible\-only
Skip cloud-init provisioning; only re-run Ansible against an existing backing image.
.TP
.B \-\-bake
Bake the image from the cloud image using the fleet XML's
.BR <bake\-name> ,
.BR <bake\-release> ,
.BR <bake\-size> ,
.BR <bake\-vcpus> ,
.BR <bake\-username> ,
.BR <bake\-password> ,
.B <bake\-packages>
and
.B <bake\-playbook>
elements, ignoring its
.BR <backing\-ref> ,
its
.BR <vm\-username> / <vm\-password>
and its
.BR <vm\-extra\-hostfwd> ,
since a bake runs QEMU on the host, where a forward whose port is taken stops
it starting. Without this flag gen\-vm pulls the fleet file's
.B <backing\-ref>
and reads none of the bake elements. The image authorises
.BR \-\-ssh\-key\-file ,
which has to be the public half of the key given to
.BR publish\-qcow . An explicit
flag still outranks an element. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-backing\-image ,
.B \-\-backing\-file
or
.BR \-\-restore\-image .
.TP
.BI \-\-backing\-image " OCI_REF"
Pull a published backing qcow2 from a container registry with
.BR oras (1),
decompress it, and create the overlay on top of it. No download from
cloud-images.ubuntu.com, no cloud-init and no bake: the same job as the
cloud-image path, from a different source. The artifact is not a runnable
image (artifactType application/vnd.batesste.vm-image.v1, one zstd layer), so
.B "docker pull"
will not fetch it. Requires
.B oras
and
.B zstd
on PATH. Mutually exclusive with
.B \-\-backing\-file
and
.BR \-\-no\-backing .
Re-running is a no-op unless
.B \-\-force
is given.
.SH GEN-COMPOSE OPTIONS
.PP
Renders
.IR docker\-compose.yml ,
.I prometheus.yml
and
.I fleet\-inventory.yml
for the fleet a fleet XML file describes. Each rocm-ernic is one node of a
TCP mesh; the first is the mesh manager and the rest are workers that dial
it.
.PP
Three stacks ship, each rendered from the fleet file of the same name:
.TP
.B rocm\-golden
.I qemu/stacks/rocm\-golden.xml
\(em two guests, each with a GPU, an NVMe, an ernic with P4 in guest scope
and an SDXI device. The default stack, and what
.B quick\-start
runs.
.TP
.B helios\-tray
.I qemu/stacks/helios\-tray.xml
\(em one guest with 4 GPUs, 8 ernics and an SDXI device.
.TP
.B mi355x\-server
.I qemu/stacks/mi355x\-server.xml
\(em one guest with 8 GPUs and 8 ernics, one of each below each of 8 PCIe
switches.
.PP
Any other fleet is one of those files plus an overlay, rendered somewhere of
your own with
.BR \-\-output\-dir .
.PP
Most elements hold one value for the whole fleet. Three take a comma-list
with one value per guest \(em
.BR <vm\-shm\-size> ,
.B <vm\-filesystem>
and
.BR <rocjitsu\-config> :
a bare value applies to every guest, a list whose length equals
.B <vm\-count>
applies positionally, and any other length is an error naming the element and
both lengths. Each distinct
.B <vm\-filesystem>
share is bind-mounted into the qemu containers once.
.B <vm\-extra\-hostfwd>
is different: its commas separate several rules for
.I one
guest, and the whole value goes to every guest.
.TP
.BI \-\-xml " FILE"
Fleet XML settings file. Repeatable; a later file overrides the elements it
sets. Default: a
.I fleet.xml
on the search path (see
.BR "SETTINGS FILE" ),
else the stack's own file in
.IR qemu/stacks/ .
The generated files carry one
.I "# xml\-sha256:"
line over all the layers concatenated, so an edit to an overlay invalidates
the render exactly as an edit to the first file does.
.TP
.BI \-\-stack " NAME"
Stack to generate:
.BR rocm\-golden ,
.B helios\-tray
or
.BR mi355x\-server .
It decides which stack's assets (Grafana dashboards, logging configuration,
the tapsetup image) are mirrored beside the render and, without
.BR \-\-output\-dir ,
where the render goes. Default: the stack named after the first
.B \-\-xml
file when that is a shipped stack, else
.BR rocm\-golden .
.TP
.BI \-\-output\-dir " DIR"
Where to write. Defaults to the resolved stack directory, except that
generating into
.I /usr/share/qemu\-tool/compose
must be asked for explicitly: that tree is owned by the Debian package.
Required for an overlay render: without it the output lands on the committed
stack named by the first
.B \-\-xml
file.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Exit non-zero if regenerating would change anything, and write nothing.
For CI.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the generated files to standard output instead of writing them.
.PP
The generated
.I docker\-compose.yml
carries a
.B "# xml\-sha256:"
header recording the fleet XML it was built from.
.B compose
recomputes it when given
.B \-\-xml
and warns on standard error when the two disagree. It never refuses to run:
.B down
against a stale tree is exactly how a bad edit is recovered from.
.PP
Some elements shape the generated fleet beyond its size:
.TP
.B <ernic\-tap>
Give the guests working Ethernet. The rocm-ernic TCP mesh carries RDMA
payload only; without a TAP the guest driver transmits, the device drops the
frames, ARP goes unanswered, and
.BR ib_send_bw (1)
cannot complete the out-of-band exchange that precedes any inter-node
transfer. With this set, each ernic is attached to a TAP that a short-lived
privileged sidecar bridges onto a shared
.I l2
network, so every guest shares one L2 segment. The sidecar holds
.B CAP_NET_ADMIN
and exits; the long-running server needs only
.IR /dev/net/tun ,
because attaching to a pre-created, owned TAP requires no capability.
Default: false.
.TP
.BR <ernic\-per\-vm> ", " <rocjitsu\-per\-vm> ", " <sdxi\-per\-vm>
Devices per guest. The first two must be at least 1. Each ernic is its own
mesh node, and the mesh stops registering workers at about 40 nodes, so 8
ernics per guest caps a fleet near 5 guests. Defaults: 1, 1 and 0.
.TP
.B <vm\-pcie\-switch\-width>
Group each guest's vfio-user devices under PCIe switches, this many per
switch; see
.B run\-vm
.BR \-\-vfio\-user\-switch\-width .
Default: 0, off.
.TP
.BR <p4\-enable> ", " <p4\-guest\-scope> ", " <p4\-program>
Run the P4 engine on each ernic server, and decide which guest commands
reach it:
.BR none ,
.B read
(counters),
.B write
(also table entries) or
.B load
(also program replacement). Defaults: false and
.BR none .
.TP
.BR <prom\-data\-dir> ", " <grafana\-data\-dir> ", " <loki\-data\-dir>
Host paths bind-mounted as the Prometheus TSDB, Grafana's state and Loki's
store. Bind mounts rather than named volumes so the record of a run is
visible to whoever made it, instead of being buried under
.I /var/lib/docker/volumes
as root and silently reused by the next fleet of a different size.
.B compose
creates the ones the fleet XML names, as the caller, before
.BR up .
.TP
.BR <grafana\-enable> ", " <logs\-enable>
Generate Grafana, and Loki with an Alloy tailing the stack's containers.
Both ride the
.B metrics
profile. Defaults: true and true.
.TP
.B <ernic\-nofile>
File-descriptor limit for every ernic service. Docker's default soft limit is
1024, which a mesh manager can reach inside a single run. Default: 65536.
.TP
.BR <log\-max\-size> ", " <log\-max\-files>
Rotation for every service's log. Docker's json-file driver is unlimited by
default, and a wedged server with no backoff on its error path can fill a
disk. Defaults: 50m and 3.
.PP
Each ernic is additionally given its own
.B ERNIC_TCP_GUEST_GIDS
naming the GIDs its guest owns \(em the EUI-64 link-local derived from the
fleet MAC, and the guest IPv4. The mesh resolves a destination GID to a node
by asking each node what it owns, so this is inherently per-node and is
derived rather than configured.
.SH ERNIC-STATS OPTIONS
.PP
Parses the stats dumps written by
.B "rocm\-ernic \-S PATH"
and serves them on
.I /metrics
in Prometheus text format. Metric and label names match rocm-ernic's own
.IR prometheus/ernic\-exporter ,
so its Grafana dashboard works against this endpoint unmodified.
.TP
.BI \-\-stats\-dir " DIR"
Directory of
.I *.stats
files. Default:
.IR /run/ernic\-stats .
.TP
.BI \-\-port " PORT"
Listen port. Default: 9840.
.TP
.BI \-\-addr " ADDR"
Listen address. Default: 0.0.0.0.
.SH COMPOSE OPTIONS
Runs
.B docker compose
against a stack. A generated stack has its fleet's shape baked into the
YAML; the only things left for
.B docker compose
to interpolate are
.BR CONTAINER_USER ,
.BR CONTAINER_KVM_GID ,
.B VM_IMAGES_DIR
and
.BR QEMU_TOOL_SRC .
.B compose
supplies those through the environment of the
.B docker compose
process \(em the caller's uid and gid, the gid that owns
.IR /dev/kvm ,
and the two paths from the fleet XML \(em and only where the caller's own
environment does not already set them. No settings file is written for
.B docker compose
or handed to it.
.TP
.BI \-\-stack " NAME"
Shipped stack to run:
.BR rocm\-golden ,
.B helios\-tray
or
.BR mi355x\-server .
Default:
.BR rocm\-golden .
.TP
.BI \-\-stack\-dir " DIR"
Run a stack rendered by
.B "gen\-compose \-\-output\-dir"
.I DIR
instead of a shipped one. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-stack ,
and required for a fleet rendered from an overlay: the guest count and the
guest names are baked into the generated YAML, so without it the shipped
stack comes up instead, reporting success.
.IP
Defaults to
.BR $QEMU_TOOL_STACK ,
and failing that to the current directory when that directory is itself a
rendered stack, the way
.B "docker compose"
picks up a
.I compose.yaml
beside you. A rendered stack is recognised by the generator's provenance
header, not by the presence of a
.IR docker\-compose.yml ,
so an unrelated docker project is never adopted. There is no walk\-up: from
anywhere in a checkout it would reach a committed stack directory and drive
it under the name of a fleet.
.TP
.BI \-\-xml " FILE"
Fleet XML settings file. Repeatable; a later file overrides the elements it
sets. See
.B SETTINGS FILE
below.
.IP
Against a rendered stack found by
.BR \-\-stack\-dir ,
.B $QEMU_TOOL_STACK
or the current directory this is optional, and without it no settings file is
read at all: the fleet's shape is in its YAML, so inspecting or stopping a
fleet needs no flags once it exists. Two values are not in the YAML. Give
.B \-\-xml
to have the images directory and the qemu\-tool source mount taken from the
fleet XML, and the render checked against the layers' current bytes; without
it
.B VM_IMAGES_DIR
is
.I /var/lib/qemu\-tool/images
and
.B QEMU_TOOL_SRC
is
.I ../../..
from the stack directory, which outside a checkout is an unrelated directory
and makes the qemu containers install qemu\-tool from PyPI. A
.I .env
in the stack directory is read only when no settings are in force. The data
directories are read from the stack's YAML and created before
.B up
either way.
.IP
For a stack named by
.B \-\-stack
the search path applies when the flag is absent, and failing that the stack's
own file in
.IR qemu/stacks/ .
Layers that are not that file are a different fleet: given with
.B \-\-xml
they draw a warning that points at
.B "gen\-compose \-\-output\-dir"
and
.BR "compose \-\-stack\-dir" .
.TP
.BI \-\-images " DIR"
Directory containing the guest images, passed as
.BR VM_IMAGES_DIR .
Default:
.B <vm\-images\-dir>
from the fleet XML, else the stack's own default,
.IR /var/lib/qemu-tool/images .
.PP
.B compose
never regenerates and never edits a settings file: resizing a fleet is an
edit to
.B <vm\-count>
followed by
.BR "gen\-compose \-\-output\-dir" ,
so a re\-render is always a decision rather than a side effect of
.BR up .
Nor is it a command runner. For a whole fleet use
.B "ansible \-i"
.I <stack>/fleet\-inventory.yml
.BR fleet ,
which
.B gen\-compose
renders beside the compose file; for one guest,
.B inspect
prints a complete
.B ssh
command, and reports the ernic netdev and its address without a login.
.PP
.B down
and
.B stop
enable every profile unless
.B \-\-profile
or
.B COMPOSE_PROFILES
scopes them, and a teardown that exits 0 while containers of its own project
are still running warns and names them.
.PP
Pass
.B \-v
to
.BR down .
Without it a killed run leaves its sockets in the shared
.I /run/vfu
volume and the next
.B up
dies on
.IR "cannot serve /run/vfu/rocjitsu\-1.sock: Address already in use" ,
seen as a dependency failing its health check.
.PP
All remaining arguments are forwarded to
.B "docker compose"
in the order typed, options included, so
.B "\-\-profile metrics up \-d"
needs no separator. This command's own options are recognised anywhere on
the line. A
.B \-\-
ends them: everything after it is docker's, and the separator itself is
dropped rather than forwarded, because
.B "docker compose"
reads one as end\-of\-options and silently ignored the profile that followed
it, bringing up the default services and reporting success.
.PP
Plain
.B docker compose
against a generated stack refuses until
.B CONTAINER_USER
and
.B CONTAINER_KVM_GID
are set, which is deliberate: unset would run every container as root.
.PP
.RS
.EX
CONTAINER_USER=$(id \-u):$(id \-g) \\
CONTAINER_KVM_GID=$(stat \-c %g /dev/kvm) docker compose up \-d
.EE
.RE
.SH LIST OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit JSON instead of the table. The JSON form additionally includes the full
disk image path, full vfio-user socket paths, the owning user, the uptime in
seconds, and an
.B nvme
array per VM whose entries carry
.BR bdf " (canonical " dddd:bb:dd.f ", or null for an emulated device whose"
guest BDF is unknown),
.BR kind " (" emulated " or " passthrough ),
.BR source " (" argv " or " qmp ),
.BR qdev_id ,
and
.BR serial " or " class_verified
respectively.
.TP
.B \-\-qemu\-tool\-only
Omit VMs that were not started by
.BR qemu\-tool .
.TP
.B \-\-summary
Replace the per-VM table with a per-fleet aggregate and a measured load
picture. VMs are grouped by Docker Compose project; uncontainerised ones are
grouped as
.BR (uncontainerised) .
Four sections are printed: a header with the service roll-call,
.B HEADROOM
(how many more VMs fit by each ceiling, and which one binds),
.B LOAD
(CPU by service class, host load average, guest RAM resident, RDMA and
Ethernet totals, GPUs, logged-in users) and
.B OUTLIERS
(VMs far off the fleet's own CPU median, occupied guests, and any container
that is not running).
.IP
Every figure has an unavailable state and prints it rather than a zero: an
unreadable collector must not look like an idle fleet. The mesh and vCPU
ceilings are the measured walls recorded in
.BR AGENTS.md " issues 17 and 14."
.IP
Costs about 7 s against a 32-VM fleet, of which 5 s is the sampling window
\(em the ernic servers rewrite their stats at 1 Hz, so a shorter window
samples that rewrite rather than a rate. Plain
.B list
is unchanged and still never contacts a guest.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-guests
With
.BR \-\-summary ,
skip the only collector that contacts a guest: the logged-in-users line,
which costs about 2.8 s over 32 VMs and runs underneath the sample window.
The users line then reads
.B "not collected"
rather than zero, and the users row of
.B OUTLIERS
is omitted. An error without
.BR \-\-summary .
.PP
.B \-\-summary
and
.B \-\-json
compose: the JSON form is one object whatever the VM count, carrying
.BR sampled_seconds ,
a
.B source
per metric group
.RB ( proc ", " cgroup ", " ernic-stats ", " guest-agent ", " unavailable )
and units in the key names
.RB ( cores_busy ", " memory_mib ", " ip_bytes_tx ).
.PP
The table reports, per VM: PID, VM name, source, architecture, vCPU count,
memory, forwarded SSH port, whether KVM is enabled, uptime, the container name
when the VM runs inside one, any attached NVMe, and any attached vfio-user
sockets.
.PP
The
.B NVME
column lists every NVMe attached to the VM, emulated or passed through,
comma-joined, with identical labels collapsed as
.IR label *N
.RB ( <vm\-nvme>2</vm\-nvme>
is ordinary, and two identical labels say no more than one plus a count), or
.B \-
when the VM has none. Four forms:
.RS
.TP
.B 00:05.0
The
.I guest
BDF of an emulated NVMe, read from the VM's QMP monitor. The
.B 0000:
domain is dropped for table width;
.B \-\-json
keeps the canonical form.
.TP
.B emul
An emulated NVMe whose guest BDF is not knowable \(em the VM has no QMP
socket, or the query failed.
.TP
.B host:03:00.0
A passed-through device, by its
.I host
BDF.
.TP
.B host:03:00.0?
The same, with no sysfs entry on this host to confirm it is an NVMe.
.RE
.PP
A host BDF and a guest BDF are
.I different address spaces,
which is why a passthrough entry is labelled rather than printed bare:
.B host:03:00.0
is the address on this machine, and the address
.BR lspci (8)
inside the guest reports is a different one. Only the guest BDF is actionable
inside the VM, and it is only available when the VM was started with
.BR \-\-qmp\-socket :
.B run\-vm
emits
.B \-device nvme,...
with no
.BR addr= ,
so QEMU picks the slot at realize time and the guest address never appears on
the command line.
.PP
Passthrough entries come from
.BR "\-device vfio\-pci,host=" ...,
which is equally how a GPU or a NIC is passed through, so the class is checked
in
.I /sys/bus/pci/devices/<bdf>/class
and only
.B 0x0108xx
(NVM Express) is claimed. A BDF this host has no sysfs entry for keeps a
trailing
.B ?
rather than being dropped, since dropping would silently hide a real NVMe.
.PP
The SSH column shows
.IR host \-> guest
whenever the two differ, which they do for a containerised VM: the guest's
.B \-hostfwd
port is the port inside that container's network namespace, and a fleet stack
gives every guest the same one. The host port \(em the one to actually
.BR ssh (1)
to \(em is the published mapping. In JSON output these are
.B ssh_port_host
and
.BR ssh_port .
An uncontainerised listing shows the single port, unchanged.
.TP
.B \-\-summary
Aggregate the listing per fleet and add a measured load picture: CPU, memory,
disk, RDMA mesh, GPUs and logins, with the headroom each one leaves. Takes a
5\ s sample, so it costs about 7\ s against a 32\-VM fleet; plain
.B list
is unchanged and still contacts no guest.
.IP
Three numbers are worth knowing before reading it. Memory is reported as the
cgroup's
.B shmem
figure, because guest RAM is a
.B memory\-backend\-memfd
and so is charged as shared memory, never to
.IR /dev/shm ;
the container's
.B anon
would understate a 32\-VM fleet twentyfold. The CPU line counts the ernic and
rocjitsu sidecars as well as QEMU, because on an emulated fleet the sidecars
burn more than the guests they serve. And GPU utilisation is reported as not
measurable rather than as a number, because the devices are emulated.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-guests
With
.BR \-\-summary ,
skip the one collector that contacts a guest \(em the logged\-in users line,
about 2.8\ s over 32 VMs. Everything else is host\-side. The users line then
reads
.I not collected
rather than zero, because nobody logged in and nobody asked are different
facts.
.SH INSPECT OPTIONS
Reports what a running VM actually has, joining the host\-side command line to
what the guest agent answers. Every field that cannot be established says so
rather than guessing: a device whose origin cannot be decided reads
.IR unknown ,
not
.IR emulated .
.PP
Each GPU row carries its PCIe BARs, indented beneath it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
GPU:       gfx1250 (emulated, rocjitsu\-1.sock)
             BAR0 256 MiB (64\-bit prefetchable)
             BAR2 2 MiB (64\-bit prefetchable)
GPU:       gfx908 (passthrough, 63:00.0)
             BAR0 32 GiB (64\-bit prefetchable)
             BAR2 2 MiB (64\-bit prefetchable)
             BAR5 512 KiB (32\-bit)
.EE
.RE
.PP
BAR0 is the VRAM aperture and is the one to look at for peer\-to\-peer DMA: a
device whose BAR0 does not cover the memory a peer must reach cannot be the
target of one. The 256 MiB above is an emulated
.BR rocjitsu (1)
GPU at its default
.IR vram_aperture_bytes ,
against a real MI100 with a 32 GiB large BAR. BAR2 and BAR5 are the doorbell
and register apertures and are the same size on both, emulated or not.
.PP
A passed\-through GPU's BARs are read from the host's own sysfs, so they are
reported whether or not the guest agent answers. An emulated one's come from
the guest, which is the only place they exist \(em so they are absent until
the guest has booted and enumerated the bus, and an absent list is never a
claim that the device has no BARs.
.TP
.I VM
Inspect a single VM, named positionally. Equivalent to
.BR \-\-vm\-name ,
which is kept because it is the spelling every other subcommand takes. Naming
the VM both ways with different values is an error rather than a precedence
rule nobody can remember.
.TP
.BI \-\-vm\-name " NAME"
Inspect a single VM. Default: every running VM.
.TP
.BI \-\-xml " FILE"
Fleet XML settings file. Overrides the search path, and is repeatable with
later files winning element by element, as everywhere else.
.TP
.B \-\-all\-devices
Expand collapsed device rows. When more than one GPU, RNIC or SDXI device is
present,
.B inspect
normally shows a single count line for each type; this flag prints every
device individually with its full BAR list.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit JSON instead of the human\-readable report. The JSON carries the same
fields, including
.BR ssh_keys " (fingerprint, comment, path and whether the guest accepts it),"
.BR block_devices ", " gpus " and " ssh_sessions .
.PP
Two fields repay explanation.
.B Key
names the key the guest will actually accept: it is read from that user's
.I authorized_keys
over the guest agent and matched against local candidates by fingerprint, so
.I accepted by guest
is a measurement rather than the configured default restated. A
.B cert\-authority
line is never offered, because a CA key signs certificates and does not log in.
.PP
.B Login
distinguishes
.IR none " from " unknown .
These guests have no
.IR /var/run/utmp ,
so
.B guest\-get\-users
answers with an empty list whether or not anyone is logged in; the answer
comes from systemd\-logind state files instead, read\-only, with no
.BR guest\-exec .
.I none
is returned only against positive evidence that a complete look happened \(em
an expired budget, a failed read or a filter that excluded an account all read
.I unknown
instead.
.SH CHECK OPTIONS
Checks this host and prints a report grouped by area. Every line is marked
.BR ok ", " warn ", " FAIL " or " skip .
A
.B FAIL
is something that stops a stack starting and sets the exit status to 1; a
.B warn
limits what the host can do without blocking it \(em a missing
.IR /dev/net/tun ,
which only an
.B <ernic\-tap>
fleet needs, or a vCPU budget past what the mesh manager's untunable 20\~s
heartbeat tolerates. A
.B skip
is a check that did not apply.
.PP
Each settings file in force is named and parsed with the same strict parser
every subcommand uses. A file that is not valid fleet XML is a
.B FAIL
with one line per problem, naming the file and the element, and the rest of
the report still runs.
.PP
The container identity is reported even when it is fine, because
.I which uid did my fleet run as
used to have a surprising answer: every stack ran as root until the
.B CONTAINER_USER
reference became unconditional. It is not a fleet XML setting: it comes from
the environment when set there, and otherwise is the caller.
.TP
.BI \-\-xml " FILE"
Fleet XML settings file to check against, repeatable, later files winning
element by element. Without it the usual search path is used, and which file
that found is itself part of the report \(em a forgotten
.I ./fleet.xml
outranks the system one, and a fleet then runs with half-remembered values.
.SH DOWN OPTIONS
Gracefully shuts down guests through the QEMU guest agent, then optionally
tears down the containing stack or all owned stacks.
.PP
This exists because stopping the container does not stop the guest.
.B run\-vm
execs QEMU, so PID 1 in a qemu container
.I is
.BR qemu\-system\-x86_64 ,
and QEMU exits on
.B SIGTERM
rather than ACPI\-shutting\-down the guest. A
.B docker compose down
is therefore a power cut: guest filesystems are left needing journal replay.
Running
.B down
first requests a clean power-off over the guest agent, then tears down the
stack.
.PP
Stack identity and ownership follow the same rules as
.B down \-\-all :
identified by the compose file's
.B gen\-compose
provenance header, never by service name or directory name.
Yours if any container in it runs as your uid.
.TP
.I TARGET
A VM name to shut down, or a stack name (the
.B STACK
column in
.BR list )
to shut down and then tear down. Default: every running VM.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Power off every running guest, then tear down every owned stack. Under
.BR sudo (8),
combine with
.B \-\-all\-users
to take every stack on the system.
.TP
.B \-\-all\-users
With
.BR \-\-all :
include stacks owned by other users. Needs root.
.TP
.B \-\-yes
Skip the teardown confirmation prompt. The prompt never defaults to yes and a
bare Enter always declines, so this is the only way to proceed unattended.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " SECONDS"
How long to wait for guests to power off. Default: 120. Exceeding it warns and
names the guests still running rather than failing \(em the caller's next step
is to stop the containers regardless, and knowing which were not clean is more
use than a refusal.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Show what would happen without doing it.
.SH MAN OPTIONS
Opens this page with
.BR man (1).
Only the Debian package installs it where
.BR man (1)
finds it unaided: the wheel ships it as package data, and neither
.BR uv " nor " pipx
has any mechanism for putting a man page on
.IR MANPATH ,
so an installed tool otherwise carries a manual nothing can read. The page is
handed to
.BR man (1)
as a file for exactly that reason.
.TP
.B \-\-path
Print the path to the bundled page and exit without opening it \(em for
piping into a pager of your own, or for adding to
.IR MANPATH .
.SH QCOW OPTIONS
Inspect and manage VM overlay qcow2 state. The three modes are mutually
exclusive; exactly one must be given.
.TP
.B \-\-list
List every VM overlay found under the image directories. Columns: Name, Path,
Virtual (advertised size), On Disk (actual blocks used), Delta (COW data
written since the overlay was created; 0\~B for a fresh overlay,
.B N/A
when there is no backing file), Age, Backing Age, Backing (backing file name),
State (running/stopped).
.TP
.BI \-\-state " VM"
Show overlay info for one VM: overlay path, virtual and on-disk sizes,
whether the image is dirty, run state, and any NVMe scratch disks.
.TP
.BI \-\-clean " VM"
Delete the overlay and all NVMe scratch images for VM, then recreate a fresh
overlay from the backing image. The VM must be stopped. Prompts for
confirmation unless
.B \-\-yes
is given.
.TP
.BR \-\-yes ", " \-y
Skip the
.B \-\-clean
confirmation prompt.
.TP
.BI \-\-images " DIR"
Directory to scan for VM images. When omitted,
.B \-\-list
scans the system images directory, the user XDG data directory,
.B $VM_IMAGES_DIR
when the environment sets it, and the
.B <vm\-images\-dir>
of a
.I fleet.xml
on the search path; the other two modes use
.BR \-\-xml 's
.B <vm\-images\-dir>
or the default directory.
.TP
.B \-\-nvme
With
.BR \-\-list :
show a separate NVMe images section listing each scratch disk by name,
on-disk size and age. Without this flag, only the total NVMe usage is shown.
.TP
.BI \-\-xml " FILE"
Fleet XML settings file. Repeatable; later files win element by element.
Its
.B <vm\-images\-dir>
is used when
.B \-\-images
is not given.
.SH PUBLISH-QCOW OPTIONS
Publish a baked guest qcow2 as an OCI artifact: the one
.B gen\-vm \-\-backing\-image
and
.B quick\-start
pull. The image is converted to a standalone qcow2 with no compressed
clusters, compressed with zstd, and pushed with
.BR oras (1).
A referrer attached to the first tag carries
.BR vm\-info.json ,
.B id_rsa
and
.BR id_rsa.pub .
What is published is the file given, so test the guest on an overlay.
.TP
.BI \-\-xml " FILE"
Fleet XML whose
.B <publish\-*>
and
.B <bake\-*>
elements become the defaults of the flags below. Repeatable; later files win.
There is no search path here: without this flag no settings file is read.
.TP
.BI \-\-image " FILE"
The baked qcow2. Refused if it has a backing file, or if
.B gen\-vm
pulled it from a registry rather than baking it. Default with
.BR \-\-xml :
.IB <vm\-images\-dir> / <bake\-name> \-backing.qcow2\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-ref " REPO"
Registry repository, without a tag. Default with
.BR \-\-xml :
.BR <publish\-ref> .
.TP
.BI \-\-tag " TAG"
Tag to push. Repeatable: one push carries them all and the referrer attaches
to the first.
.TP
.BI \-\-key " FILE"
Private key the guest authorises;
.IB FILE .pub
must sit beside it. The keypair is published, so generate one for the image
and never pass a personal key. Required: it is never taken from a settings
file, not even from
.BR <vm\-ssh\-key\-file> ,
which is a fleet's own login key. Refused when it is not the key
.B gen\-vm
recorded baking into the image; bake with
.B \-\-ssh\-key\-file
.IB FILE .pub\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-vm\-name " NAME"
Names the qcow2 inside the artifact. Default with
.BR \-\-xml :
.BR <bake\-name> .
.TP
.BI \-\-flavour " NAME"
Recorded in vm\-info.json and the annotations. Default:
.BR <publish\-flavour> ,
else
.BR rocm\-golden .
.TP
.BI \-\-username " NAME"
The guest login. Default:
.BR <bake\-username> ,
else
.BR ubuntu .
.TP
.BI \-\-password " PASSWORD"
Recorded in vm\-info.json and never in an annotation. Default:
.BR <bake\-password> ,
else
.BR password .
.TP
.BI \-\-release " NAME"
Ubuntu codename of the guest. Default:
.BR <bake\-release> ,
else
.BR resolute .
.TP
.BI \-\-architecture " ARCH"
Default
.BR amd64 .
.TP
.BI \-\-guest\-stamp " FILE"
A copy of the guest's
.BR /etc/rocm\-golden\-guest.json .
vm\-info.json takes the kernel and the pins from it and carries it whole.
.TP
.BI \-\-playbook " NAME"
The playbook the guest was baked with, for the record. Default:
.BR <bake\-playbook> .
.TP
.BI \-\-commit " SHA"
The qemu\-minimal commit the guest was baked from.
.TP
.BI \-\-source " URL"
Source repository for the
.B org.opencontainers.image.source
annotation.
.TP
.BI \-\-work\-dir " DIR"
Where the copy is made. Default: beside the image. Needs room for the whole
disk.
.TP
.BI \-\-digest\-file " FILE"
Write the pushed reference, with its digest, to FILE.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Convert and compress, print what would be pushed, and push nothing. Needs no
.B oras
and no credentials.
.TP
.BI \-\-keep " DIR"
With
.BR \-\-dry\-run :
keep the compressed image and vm\-info.json in DIR.
.SH SSH OPTIONS
Opens an SSH session to a running VM, resolving the port, username and key
through exactly the same path
.B inspect
prints them \(em the username from the image's own sidecar rather than the
configured default, and an identity only when the guest names a key whose
private half is on this host. When it does not, no
.B \-i
is passed and a note says why before the password prompt appears; offering a
key the guest does not accept only buys a refusal.
.PP
Everything after the VM name goes to
.BR ssh (1),
so this tool's own options come
.I before
it \(em the order
.BR ssh (1)
and
.B docker exec
also take.
.TP
.I VM
Name of the running VM. Required.
.TP
.BI \-\-xml " FILE"
Fleet XML settings file. Repeatable; later files win.
.TP
.B \-\-strict\-host\-key
Keep
.BR ssh (1)'s
own host\-key checking, which is off by default. Every guest built from one
backing image carries the
.I same
host key, and a fleet hands them consecutive ports on
.IR localhost ,
so port 2222 names a different machine on every fleet. Strict checking
therefore fails with
.I REMOTE HOST IDENTIFICATION HAS CHANGED
as a matter of course, and recording these in
.I known_hosts
would poison the entry for whatever uses that port next. The generated
.I fleet\-inventory.yml
passes the same pair, for the same reason.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the
.BR ssh (1)
command instead of running it.
.PP
To run a remote command, separate it with
.BR \-\- :
.PP
.EX
qemu\-tool ssh rocm\-golden\-1 \-\- ibv_devinfo \-l
.EE
.SH VM IDENTIFICATION
.B qemu\-tool
stamps every VM it launches with two generic QEMU options:
.PP
.RS
.EX
\-name guest=<vm\-name>,debug\-threads=on
\-uuid <uuid5(namespace, "<role>:<vm\-name>")>
.EE
.RE
.PP
where
.I role
is
.B run\-vm
or
.BR gen\-vm .
.B list
recomputes the UUID from the parsed guest name and compares it against the
.B \-uuid
on the command line. An exact match identifies the VM as
.BR qemu\-tool 's
and reveals which subcommand started it, so a transient
.B gen\-vm
image-build VM is distinguishable from a running
.B run\-vm
VM.
.PP
Both options are generic rather than machine-specific, so they are accepted on
all supported architectures, including
.B riscv64
where
.B \-smbios
is not.
.PP
VMs shown as
.B external
were either started by something other than
.BR qemu\-tool ,
or were started by a version predating these markers; restart such a VM for it
to be identified. Identification is for operator convenience, not a security
boundary \(em the markers are plain command-line arguments and can be set by
anyone.
.SH SETTINGS FILE
Fleet XML is the only settings format. A file is one
.B <fleet>
element in the namespace
.I http://qemu\-tool/fleet/1.0
whose children each hold one setting:
.PP
.RS
.EX
<fleet xmlns="http://qemu\-tool/fleet/1.0">
  <vm\-count>2</vm\-count>
  <vm\-name\-prefix>rocm\-golden</vm\-name\-prefix>
  <vm\-vcpus>4</vm\-vcpus>
  <vm\-images\-dir>${HOME}/images</vm\-images\-dir>
</fleet>
.EE
.RE
.PP
.I qemu/stacks/rocm\-golden.xml
is the annotated reference. An element that is absent keeps its default.
The parser is strict: an element it does not know, an element given twice in
one file, an attribute, a nested element and text directly under
.B <fleet>
are each an error that names the file and the element, and for an unknown
element the closest known name. XML comments are allowed anywhere. A misspelt
element is therefore refused, not read as a setting left at its default.
.B ${VAR}
is expanded from the environment in the path elements only:
.BR <vm\-images\-dir> ,
.BR <vm\-ssh\-key\-file> ,
.BR <bake\-packages> ,
.B <bake\-playbook>
and the three data directories.
.SS Layers
.B \-\-xml
is repeatable. The files are read left to right and a later file overrides
only the elements it sets, so an overlay is a short file naming what differs:
.PP
.RS
.EX
qemu\-tool gen\-compose \-\-xml qemu/stacks/rocm\-golden.xml \-\-xml one\-guest.xml \\
    \-\-output\-dir /var/lib/qemu\-tool/stacks/one\-guest
.EE
.RE
.SS Search path
With no
.BR \-\-xml ,
the first of these that exists is used, alone:
.BR $QEMU_TOOL_FLEET ,
.IR ./fleet.xml ,
.I qemu/stacks/fleet.xml
in a source checkout,
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/qemu\-tool/fleet.xml ,
.IR /etc/qemu\-tool/fleet.xml .
None of these is created for you; the Debian package leaves
.I /etc/qemu\-tool/
empty, because a file there is the settings of every command given no
.BR \-\-xml .
With none, the built-in defaults apply. Only
.B \-\-xml
layers: the search path resolves to exactly one file, so layering is
something asked for on the command line and never something inherited from
the ambient environment. Four commands differ.
.B gen\-compose
and
.B "compose \-\-stack"
fall back to the stack's own file in
.I qemu/stacks/
when the search finds nothing;
.B quick\-start
does not search and uses its flavor's file;
.B publish\-qcow
reads no settings file unless
.B \-\-xml
names one.
.SS Per-guest settings
.B gen\-vm
and
.B run\-vm
take these elements as the defaults of their options. Values are applied
lowest to highest from the built-in defaults, a
.B \-\-domain
XML, the fleet XML, then explicit command-line options, so an option always
beats the file.
.PP
.RS
.EX
<vm\-vcpus>                    \-\-vcpus
<vm\-vmem>                     \-\-vmem
<vm\-images\-dir>               \-\-images
<vm\-nvme>                     \-\-nvme
<vm\-filesystem>               \-\-filesystem
<vm\-extra\-hostfwd>            \-\-extra\-hostfwd
<vm\-backing\-shared>           \-\-backing\-shared
<vm\-vfio\-user\-root\-port>      \-\-vfio\-user\-root\-port
<vm\-cpu\-sockets>              \-\-cpu\-sockets
<vm\-cpu\-phys\-bits>            \-\-cpu\-phys\-bits
<vm\-pci\-hole64\-size>          \-\-pci\-hole64\-size
<vm\-username>                 \-\-username
<vm\-password>                 \-\-password
<vm\-ssh\-key\-file>             \-\-ssh\-key\-file
<backing\-ref>                 \-\-backing\-image
.EE
.RE
.PP
Everything else those two commands take is a flag only.
.SS Fleet settings
Read by
.BR gen\-compose ,
.B compose
and
.BR quick\-start .
.TP
Shape
.BR <vm\-count> ,
.BR <vm\-name\-prefix> ,
.BR <ssh\-port\-base> ,
.BR <stop\-grace\-period> ,
.BR <domain\-pattern> .
.TP
Images
.BR <qemu\-ref> ,
.BR <ernic\-ref> ,
.BR <rocjitsu\-ref> ,
.BR <sdxi\-image> ,
.BR <backing\-ref> ,
the optional
.BR <qemu\-digest> ,
.BR <ernic\-digest> ,
.B <rocjitsu\-digest>
and
.B <backing\-digest>
beside the reference each pins, and
.BR <image\-allow\-guest\-lag> ,
which excuses
.B <backing\-ref>
from the build-stamp check.
.TP
Devices
.BR <ernic\-per\-vm> ,
.BR <rocjitsu\-per\-vm> ,
.BR <sdxi\-per\-vm> ,
.BR <vm\-pcie\-switch\-width> ,
.BR <rocjitsu\-config> .
.TP
Network and mesh
.BR <fleet\-prefix> ,
.BR <ernic\-subnet> ,
.BR <ernic\-mac\-prefix> ,
.BR <ernic\-tap> ,
.BR <ernic\-tcp\-port> ,
.BR <ernic\-stats\-port> ,
.BR <ernic\-nofile> ,
.BR <p4\-enable> ,
.BR <p4\-guest\-scope> ,
.BR <p4\-program> .
.TP
Guests
the per-guest elements above, and
.BR <vm\-shm\-size> .
.TP
Metrics and logs
.BR <prom\-port> ,
.BR <prom\-scrape\-interval> ,
.BR <prom\-retention> ,
.BR <prom\-data\-dir> ,
.BR <publish\-bind\-addr> ,
.BR <grafana\-enable> ,
.BR <grafana\-image> ,
.BR <grafana\-port> ,
.BR <grafana\-admin\-password> ,
.BR <grafana\-data\-dir> ,
.BR <logs\-enable> ,
.BR <loki\-image> ,
.BR <loki\-port> ,
.BR <loki\-data\-dir> ,
.BR <alloy\-image> ,
.BR <log\-max\-size> ,
.BR <log\-max\-files> .
.TP
Paths
.BR <qemu\-tool\-src> ,
the checkout or install the qemu containers mount.
.SS Bake and publish settings
Read only by
.B "gen\-vm \-\-bake"
and
.BR "publish\-qcow \-\-xml" .
A plain
.B gen\-vm
pulls the fleet file's
.B <backing\-ref>
and reads none of these.
.B <backing\-ref>
and
.B <publish\-ref>
may name different repositories; in
.I rocm\-golden.xml
they do today.
.TP
.B <bake\-name>
Name of the baked guest; the image is
.IR <vm\-images\-dir>/<bake\-name>\-backing.qcow2 .
.TP
.BR <bake\-release> ", " <bake\-size> ", " <bake\-vcpus>
Ubuntu release, disk size in GB and the vCPUs the bake runs with.
.TP
.BR <bake\-packages> ", " <bake\-playbook>
Package manifest path (or
.BR none )
and Ansible playbook path, relative to the working directory. A
.B ~
is expanded.
.TP
.BR <bake\-username> ", " <bake\-password>
The account baked into the image. Separate from
.BR <vm\-username> / <vm\-password>
because these are published in
.IR vm\-info.json ;
a fleet's own login never reaches a registry. Unset, they fall to
.BR ubuntu / password ,
never to the fleet's login.
.TP
.BR <publish\-ref> ", " <publish\-flavour>
Registry repository, without a tag, and the flavour recorded with the image.
.SS What is not a fleet XML setting
.B CONTAINER_USER
and
.B CONTAINER_KVM_GID
differ per host and so are never in a file: they come from the environment
when set there, and otherwise from the caller's ids and the owner of
.IR /dev/kvm .
One-shot actions
.RB ( \-\-dry\-run ,
.BR \-\-force ,
.BR \-\-restore\-image ,
.BR \-\-ansible\-only ,
.BR \-\-nvme\-recreate )
are flags only.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B QEMU_TOOL_FLEET
A fleet XML file, first on the search path.
.TP
.B QEMU_TOOL_STACK
A rendered stack directory for
.B compose
to drive when
.B \-\-stack\-dir
is not given.
.TP
.BR CONTAINER_USER ", " CONTAINER_KVM_GID
The
.I uid:gid
the containers run as and the gid that opens
.IR /dev/kvm .
Set either to override what
.B compose
and
.B quick\-start
would derive from the caller.
.TP
.B VM_IMAGES_DIR
Interpolated by the generated stacks. Set by
.B compose
from
.B \-\-images
or
.BR <vm\-images\-dir> ;
a value already in the environment wins over the fleet XML.
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " XDG_DATA_HOME ", " XDG_STATE_HOME
Where the per-user
.IR fleet.xml ,
the fallback images directory and
.BR quick\-start 's
fallback stack directory live.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/qemu-tool/fleet.xml
Site settings file, last on the search path. Nothing creates it: an admin copies
.I /usr/share/qemu-tool/stacks/rocm\-golden.xml
there when the site wants one.
.TP
.I /usr/share/qemu-tool/stacks/
The fleet file of each shipped stack:
.IR rocm\-golden.xml ,
.I helios\-tray.xml
and
.IR mi355x\-server.xml .
.I rocm\-golden.xml
documents the elements.
.TP
.I /usr/share/qemu-tool/compose/
The generated stack directories of the same names, used by
.BR compose .
Their
.IR docker\-compose.yml ,
.I prometheus.yml
and
.I fleet\-inventory.yml
are written by
.BR gen\-compose ;
edit the fleet file, not them.
.TP
.I /var/lib/qemu-tool/images
Default directory for VM image files.
Owned by
.B root:kvm
with permissions
.B 2775
(setgid so new files inherit the
.B kvm
group).
Used only when it is writable; otherwise
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/qemu-tool/images
is used instead, so an unprivileged install still has somewhere to write.
.TP
.I /var/lib/qemu-tool/stacks/
Where
.B quick\-start
renders its stacks, and a conventional place for your own.
.TP
.I /usr/share/qemu-tool/packages-default
Default package manifest installed into new VMs by
.BR gen\-vm .
.PP
The package manifests, the fleet files and the compose stacks are also
bundled inside the Python distribution, so
.B "uv tool install qemu-tool"
(or
.BR "pipx install qemu-tool" )
resolves them with no system install present. The
.I /usr/share/qemu-tool
copies take precedence whenever they exist.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create a VM named
.I myvm
using the Noble release:
.PP
.RS
.EX
qemu\-tool gen\-vm \-\-vm\-name myvm \-\-release noble
.EE
.RE
.PP
Launch the VM:
.PP
.RS
.EX
qemu\-tool run\-vm \-\-vm\-name myvm
.EE
.RE
.PP
Dry-run with two NVMe drives and KVM disabled:
.PP
.RS
.EX
qemu\-tool run\-vm \-\-vm\-name myvm \-\-nvme 2 \-\-no\-kvm \-\-dry\-run
.EE
.RE
.PP
Emit a libvirt domain XML:
.PP
.RS
.EX
qemu\-tool run\-vm \-\-vm\-name myvm \-\-convert\-to\-libvirt myvm.xml
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check this host, then bring the default two-guest stack up and test it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
qemu\-tool quick\-start preflight
qemu\-tool quick\-start
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run the shipped default stack by hand, with metrics. Fetch the published
backing image once, overlay the second guest on it, start the stack:
.PP
.RS
.EX
qemu\-tool gen\-vm \-\-xml qemu/stacks/rocm\-golden.xml \-\-vm\-name rocm\-golden\-1
qemu\-tool gen\-vm \-\-xml qemu/stacks/rocm\-golden.xml \-\-vm\-name rocm\-golden\-2 \\
    \-\-backing\-file /var/lib/qemu\-tool/images/rocm\-golden\-1\-backing.qcow2
qemu\-tool compose \-\-xml qemu/stacks/rocm\-golden.xml \\
    \-\-stack rocm\-golden \-\-profile metrics up \-d
.EE
.RE
.PP
Re-render a shipped stack after editing its fleet file, and confirm the
committed stack is in step with it, for CI:
.PP
.RS
.EX
qemu\-tool gen\-compose \-\-xml qemu/stacks/rocm\-golden.xml
qemu\-tool gen\-compose \-\-xml qemu/stacks/rocm\-golden.xml \-\-check
.EE
.RE
.PP
Render and run a fleet of your own by layering an overlay over a shipped
fleet file. Only the elements the overlay states change:
.PP
.RS
.EX
qemu\-tool gen\-compose \\
    \-\-xml qemu/stacks/rocm\-golden.xml \-\-xml my\-overlay.xml \\
    \-\-output\-dir /var/lib/qemu\-tool/stacks/mine
qemu\-tool compose \-\-stack\-dir /var/lib/qemu\-tool/stacks/mine \\
    \-\-xml qemu/stacks/rocm\-golden.xml \-\-xml my\-overlay.xml \\
    \-\-profile metrics up \-d
cd /var/lib/qemu\-tool/stacks/mine && qemu\-tool compose ps
.EE
.RE
.PP
Both directory flags are required for an overlay fleet.
.B \-\-output\-dir
keeps the render off the committed stack, which
.B gen\-compose
writes by default and
.B \-\-check
compares against; and
.B \-\-stack\-dir
is what runs the render, since the guest count and the guest names are baked
into the generated YAML rather than read from the settings layers at run
time.
.PP
Bake the guest image the default fleet describes, then publish it. The key
is published with the image, so it is one generated for it, and the bake is
told to authorise it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
ssh\-keygen \-t rsa \-N "" \-f ./image\-key/id_rsa
qemu\-tool gen\-vm \-\-bake \-\-xml qemu/stacks/rocm\-golden.xml \\
    \-\-ssh\-key\-file ./image\-key/id_rsa.pub
qemu\-tool publish\-qcow \-\-xml qemu/stacks/rocm\-golden.xml \\
    \-\-tag 20261116.gabc1234 \-\-key ./image\-key/id_rsa \-\-dry\-run
.EE
.RE
.PP
List every VM running on this node:
.PP
.RS
.EX
qemu\-tool list
.EE
.RE
.PP
List only VMs this tool started, as JSON:
.PP
.RS
.EX
qemu\-tool list \-\-qemu\-tool\-only \-\-json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Find the PID of a VM by name:
.PP
.RS
.EX
qemu\-tool list \-\-json | jq \-r '.[] | select(.name=="myvm") | .pid'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check stack status, then shut the guests down cleanly and tear the stack
down:
.PP
.RS
.EX
qemu\-tool compose \-\-stack rocm\-golden ps
qemu\-tool down rocm\-golden
.EE
.RE
